@prefix ex: <https://w3id.org/project-chemie-digital/resource/> .
@prefix skos: <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#> .
@prefix dct: <http://purl.org/dc/terms/> .

<https://w3id.org/project-chemie-digital/graph/knowledge/level5-student-t> {
  ex:l5-student-t a cd:Concept ; skos:prefLabel "Student-t inference under iid normal sampling"@en ; cd:hasDefinition ex:l5-t16-definition ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l5-t16-definition a cd:Definition ; cd:body "For independent identically distributed normal observations with positive population variance, the sample mean standardized by its estimated standard error has a Student-t distribution with sample size minus one degrees of freedom. The sample mean and sample variance are independent under this model. Replacing known population SD by random sample SD changes the reference distribution. Exact small-sample t inference requires the normal sampling assumptions; a small estimated SE does not establish them."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l5-t16a-formula a cd:MathExpression ; cd:formulaLabel "An exact distribution under iid normal sampling"@en ; cd:body "Sample variance is random. Sample size minus one times sample variance, divided by population variance, has a chi-square distribution with sample size minus one degrees of freedom."@en ; cd:latex "U=\\frac{(n-1)S^2}{\\sigma^2}\\sim\\chi^2_{n-1}" ; dct:hasPart ex:l5-t16a-legend ; cd:hasSymbol ex:l5-t16a-variance, ex:l5-t16a-population, ex:l5-t16a-size, ex:l5-t16a-law ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l5-t16a-legend a cd:DefinitionList ; cd:hasDefinitionListEntry ex:l5-t16a-entry ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l5-t16a-entry a cd:DefinitionListEntry ; cd:position 1 ; skos:prefLabel "Symbols, scaling and assumptions"@en ; dct:references ex:l5-t16a-variance, ex:l5-t16a-population, ex:l5-t16a-size, ex:l5-t16a-law ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l5-t16a-variance a cd:MathSymbol ; cd:symbol "random variance" ; cd:mathNotation "S^2" ; cd:body "Random sample variance, using the sample mean and divisor n minus 1. A new sample gives a new value, in (mg/L) squared."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l5-t16a-population a cd:MathSymbol ; cd:symbol "population variance" ; cd:mathNotation "\\sigma^2" ; cd:body "Fixed positive individual population variance, 0.10 (mg/L) squared in this specified normal model. Not each sample's observed variance."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l5-t16a-size a cd:MathSymbol ; cd:symbol "size and scaling" ; cd:mathNotation "n,\\ n-1,\\ U" ; cd:body "Sample size n = 5; n minus 1 = 4 degrees of freedom. Multiply sample variance by 4 and divide by population variance: U has no units."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l5-t16a-law a cd:MathSymbol ; cd:symbol "distribution notation" ; cd:mathNotation "=,\\ \\sim,\\ \\chi^2_{n-1}" ; cd:body "Equality defines U; tilde means distributed as. Chi-square is a distribution name; subscript is degrees of freedom. Exact here for iid normal individuals."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l5-t16b-formula a cd:MathExpression ; cd:formulaLabel "Chi-square as a sum of squared standard normals"@en ; cd:body "The sum of squares of nu independent standard-normal random variables has a chi-square distribution with nu degrees of freedom."@en ; cd:latex "U=\\sum_{i=1}^{\\nu}Z_i^2\\sim\\chi^2_{\\nu}" ; dct:hasPart ex:l5-t16b-legend ; cd:hasSymbol ex:l5-t16b-normals, ex:l5-t16b-index, ex:l5-t16b-sum, ex:l5-t16b-law ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l5-t16b-legend a cd:DefinitionList ; cd:hasDefinitionListEntry ex:l5-t16b-entry ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l5-t16b-entry a cd:DefinitionListEntry ; cd:position 1 ; skos:prefLabel "Symbols and construction"@en ; dct:references ex:l5-t16b-normals, ex:l5-t16b-index, ex:l5-t16b-sum, ex:l5-t16b-law ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l5-t16b-normals a cd:MathSymbol ; cd:symbol "standard normals" ; cd:mathNotation "Z_i" ; cd:body "Independent identically distributed standard-normal variables, each with mean 0 and variance 1. They are dimensionless."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l5-t16b-index a cd:MathSymbol ; cd:symbol "index and degrees of freedom" ; cd:mathNotation "i,\\ \\nu" ; cd:body "Index i runs from 1 to positive integer nu, the number of independent squared terms. The plotted example has nu = 4."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l5-t16b-sum a cd:MathSymbol ; cd:symbol "sum of squares" ; cd:mathNotation "U,\\ \\sum_{i=1}^{\\nu},\\ {}^2" ; cd:body "Square each standard normal and add all nu terms to obtain nonnegative U. U and its density axis are dimensionless."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l5-t16b-law a cd:MathSymbol ; cd:symbol "law and equality" ; cd:mathNotation "=,\\ \\sim,\\ \\chi^2_{\\nu}" ; cd:body "Equality defines the sum. Tilde means distributed as chi-square with nu degrees of freedom, not equality to a density curve."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l5-t16c-formula a cd:MathExpression ; cd:formulaLabel "Known population SD"@en ; cd:body "Under iid normal sampling, the mean minus the population mean, divided by population SD over square root of size, is exactly standard normal."@en ; cd:latex "Z=\\frac{\\bar X-\\mu}{\\sigma/\\sqrt n}\\sim\\mathcal N(0,1)" ; dct:hasPart ex:l5-t16c-legend ; cd:hasSymbol ex:l5-t16c-mean, ex:l5-t16c-sd, ex:l5-t16c-scaling, ex:l5-t16c-law ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l5-t16c-legend a cd:DefinitionList ; cd:hasDefinitionListEntry ex:l5-t16c-entry ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l5-t16c-entry a cd:DefinitionListEntry ; cd:position 1 ; skos:prefLabel "Symbols for the known-SD reference"@en ; dct:references ex:l5-t16c-mean, ex:l5-t16c-sd, ex:l5-t16c-scaling, ex:l5-t16c-law ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l5-t16c-mean a cd:MathSymbol ; cd:symbol "random mean and population mean" ; cd:mathNotation "\\bar X,\\ \\mu" ; cd:body "Random sample mean and fixed population expectation. Both have concentration units mg/L; subtraction centers the mean."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l5-t16c-sd a cd:MathSymbol ; cd:symbol "known SD" ; cd:mathNotation "\\sigma,\\ n" ; cd:body "Known positive individual population SD in mg/L; n is sample size. Under iid sampling, sigma divided by root n is the true SE."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l5-t16c-scaling a cd:MathSymbol ; cd:symbol "standardization" ; cd:mathNotation "Z,\\ -,\\ /,\\ \\sqrt n,\\ =" ; cd:body "Subtract, then divide by the SD of the mean. The root is positive; equality defines dimensionless Z. This denominator is not estimated."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l5-t16c-law a cd:MathSymbol ; cd:symbol "standard normal law" ; cd:mathNotation "\\sim,\\ \\mathcal N(0,1)" ; cd:body "Tilde means distributed as normal with mean 0 and variance 1. Exact for iid normal observations at every positive sample size."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l5-t16d-formula a cd:MathExpression ; cd:formulaLabel "Unknown population SD: a random denominator"@en ; cd:body "Under iid normal sampling, replacing population SD by sample SD gives a Student-t statistic with sample size minus one degrees of freedom."@en ; cd:latex "T=\\frac{\\bar X-\\mu}{S/\\sqrt n}\\sim t_{n-1}" ; dct:hasPart ex:l5-t16d-legend ; cd:hasSymbol ex:l5-t16d-mean, ex:l5-t16d-sd, ex:l5-t16d-scaling, ex:l5-t16d-law ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l5-t16d-legend a cd:DefinitionList ; cd:hasDefinitionListEntry ex:l5-t16d-entry ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l5-t16d-entry a cd:DefinitionListEntry ; cd:position 1 ; skos:prefLabel "Symbols for the estimated-SD reference"@en ; dct:references ex:l5-t16d-mean, ex:l5-t16d-sd, ex:l5-t16d-scaling, ex:l5-t16d-law ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l5-t16d-mean a cd:MathSymbol ; cd:symbol "random mean and fixed expectation" ; cd:mathNotation "\\bar X,\\ \\mu" ; cd:body "Random sample mean and fixed population mean in mg/L. Their difference is centered at zero under the model."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l5-t16d-sd a cd:MathSymbol ; cd:symbol "random sample SD" ; cd:mathNotation "S,\\ S/\\sqrt n" ; cd:body "Uppercase S is random sample SD, using divisor n minus 1 for variance. Dividing by positive root n estimates the mean's SE, in mg/L."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l5-t16d-scaling a cd:MathSymbol ; cd:symbol "statistic and arithmetic" ; cd:mathNotation "T,\\ n,\\ -,\\ /,\\ =" ; cd:body "n is sample size, at least 2. Subtract the population mean and divide by the estimated SE. Equality defines dimensionless T."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l5-t16d-law a cd:MathSymbol ; cd:symbol "Student-t law" ; cd:mathNotation "\\sim,\\ t_{n-1}" ; cd:body "Tilde means distributed as Student-t; subscript n minus 1 is degrees of freedom. Exact for iid normal individuals, not arbitrary five-value samples."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l5-t16g-formula a cd:MathExpression ; cd:formulaLabel "Two-sided 95% t interval for the mean"@en ; cd:body "The observed mean plus or minus the 0.975 Student-t quantile with four degrees of freedom times the estimated SE gives approximately 9.60735 to 10.39265 mg/L."@en ; cd:latex "\\begin{gathered}\\bar x\\pm t_{0.975;4}\\frac{s}{\\sqrt n}\\\\\\approx[9.60735,\\ 10.39265]\\;\\mathrm{mg/L}\\end{gathered}" ; dct:hasPart ex:l5-t16g-legend ; cd:hasSymbol ex:l5-t16g-data, ex:l5-t16g-quantile, ex:l5-t16g-margin, ex:l5-t16g-interval ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l5-t16g-legend a cd:DefinitionList ; cd:hasDefinitionListEntry ex:l5-t16g-entry ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l5-t16g-entry a cd:DefinitionListEntry ; cd:position 1 ; skos:prefLabel "Symbols, quantile and interval convention"@en ; dct:references ex:l5-t16g-data, ex:l5-t16g-quantile, ex:l5-t16g-margin, ex:l5-t16g-interval ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l5-t16g-data a cd:MathSymbol ; cd:symbol "observed laboratory series" ; cd:mathNotation "\\bar x,\\ s,\\ n" ; cd:body "Laboratory mean 10.0 mg/L, SD the root of 0.10 (mg/L) squared, and n = 5 repeats of one sample. Not five environmental sampling units."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l5-t16g-quantile a cd:MathSymbol ; cd:symbol "t quantile" ; cd:mathNotation "t_{0.975;4}" ; cd:body "Student-t quantile about 2.776445. Subscripts: cumulative probability 0.975 and 4 degrees of freedom. Symmetry leaves 2.5% in each tail."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l5-t16g-margin a cd:MathSymbol ; cd:symbol "margin and root" ; cd:mathNotation "\\pm,\\ s/\\sqrt n" ; cd:body "Positive square root of size; estimated SE 0.141421 mg/L. Multiply by the quantile for margin 0.392649 mg/L; plus/minus gives both endpoints."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l5-t16g-interval a cd:MathSymbol ; cd:symbol "interval notation" ; cd:mathNotation "\\approx,\\ [\\ ,\\ ]" ; cd:body "Rounded inclusive endpoints in mg/L. Interval for the analytical expectation, not the water-body mean, individual results or total measurement uncertainty."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
}

<https://w3id.org/project-chemie-digital/graph/specifications/level5-student-t> {
  ex:unit-level5-probability-models cd:hasFocusConcept ex:l5-student-t .
  ex:l5-t16a-title a cd:Interpretation ; skos:prefLabel "Use chi-square to describe variance uncertainty"@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l5-t16b-title a cd:Interpretation ; skos:prefLabel "Why the variance reference has this form"@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l5-t16c-title a cd:Interpretation ; skos:prefLabel "Mean uncertainty if the component SD is known"@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l5-t16d-title a cd:Interpretation ; skos:prefLabel "Estimating SD adds uncertainty to the mean"@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l5-t16e-title a cd:Interpretation ; skos:prefLabel "Few observations make SD less certain"@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l5-t16f-title a cd:Interpretation ; skos:prefLabel "More information stabilizes the SD estimate"@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l5-t16g-title a cd:Interpretation ; skos:prefLabel "Interval for one sample's analytical mean"@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l5-t16a-chart a cd:ChartDefinition ; skos:prefLabel "First five variances from S15's independent normal laboratory series"@en ; cd:body "Five measurements per laboratory series; model variance 0.10 (mg/L) squared."@en ; cd:chartType cd:BarChart ; cd:chartPresentation "complete" ; cd:usesDataset ex:l5-t16-variances ; cd:xAxisLabel "Laboratory series"@en ; cd:yAxisLabel "Sample variance"@en ; cd:yAxisUnit "(mg/L)^2" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l5-t16-variances a cd:Dataset ; skos:prefLabel "First five realized sample variances from the S15 protocol"@en ; cd:derivedFromResource ex:l5-t16-protocol ; cd:hasObservation ex:l5-t16-v1, ex:l5-t16-v2, ex:l5-t16-v3, ex:l5-t16-v4, ex:l5-t16-v5 ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l5-t16-v1 a cd:Observation ; cd:position 1 ; skos:prefLabel "1"@en ; cd:numericValue 0.052171349805719004 ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l5-t16-v2 a cd:Observation ; cd:position 2 ; skos:prefLabel "2"@en ; cd:numericValue 0.014276595004849672 ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l5-t16-v3 a cd:Observation ; cd:position 3 ; skos:prefLabel "3"@en ; cd:numericValue 0.043448180554987004 ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l5-t16-v4 a cd:Observation ; cd:position 4 ; skos:prefLabel "4"@en ; cd:numericValue 0.03828844895421902 ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l5-t16-v5 a cd:Observation ; cd:position 5 ; skos:prefLabel "5"@en ; cd:numericValue 0.06766674384377262 ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l5-t16b-chart a cd:ChartDefinition ; skos:prefLabel "Theoretical chi-square density, four degrees of freedom"@en ; cd:body "Four independent squared standard normals give this law. Not a fit to five bars. Right tail continues beyond 32."@en ; cd:chartType cd:LineChart ; cd:chartPresentation "complete" ; cd:showChartPoints false ; cd:chartYMinimum 0.0 ; cd:chartYMaximum 0.20 ; cd:usesDataset ex:l5-t16-chi4 ; cd:xAxisLabel "Scaled variance"@en ; cd:xAxisUnit "1" ; cd:yAxisLabel "Density"@en ; cd:yAxisUnit "1" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l5-t16c-chart a cd:ChartDefinition ; skos:prefLabel "Exact standard-normal reference with known population SD"@en ; cd:body "Known SD: standard normal. Under iid normal sampling this is exact, not a large-sample approximation. Both tails continue."@en ; cd:chartType cd:LineChart ; cd:chartPresentation "complete" ; cd:showChartPoints false ; cd:chartYMinimum 0.0 ; cd:chartYMaximum 0.42 ; cd:usesDataset ex:l5-c14-standard-normal ; cd:xAxisLabel "Standardized mean"@en ; cd:xAxisUnit "1" ; cd:yAxisLabel "Density"@en ; cd:yAxisUnit "1" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l5-t16d-chart a cd:ChartDefinition ; skos:prefLabel "Student-t with four degrees of freedom versus standard normal"@en ; cd:body "Solid: normal. Dashed: t with 4 degrees of freedom. Estimating SD changes the law; heavier tails continue beyond the window."@en ; cd:chartType cd:LineChart ; cd:chartPresentation "complete" ; cd:showChartPoints false ; cd:chartYMinimum 0.0 ; cd:chartYMaximum 0.42 ; cd:usesDataset ex:l5-c14-standard-normal ; dct:hasPart ex:l5-t16-t4 ; cd:xAxisLabel "Standardized statistic"@en ; cd:xAxisUnit "1" ; cd:yAxisLabel "Density"@en ; cd:yAxisUnit "1" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l5-t16e-context a cd:Interpretation ; cd:body "Estimating spread from few observations gives a heavier-tailed reference than standard normal."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l5-t16f-context a cd:Interpretation ; cd:body "As degrees of freedom increase, Student-t approaches standard normal, but is not identical at a finite value."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l5-t16e-chart a cd:ChartDefinition ; skos:prefLabel "Normal versus Student-t with 2 and 4 degrees of freedom"@en ; cd:body "Solid: normal. Dashed: 2 degrees of freedom. Dotted: 4. Same axes on both comparisons; tails extend beyond -6 to 6."@en ; cd:chartType cd:LineChart ; cd:chartPresentation "complete" ; cd:showChartPoints false ; cd:chartYMinimum 0.0 ; cd:chartYMaximum 0.42 ; cd:usesDataset ex:l5-c14-standard-normal ; dct:hasPart ex:l5-t16-t2, ex:l5-t16-t4 ; cd:xAxisLabel "Standardized statistic"@en ; cd:xAxisUnit "1" ; cd:yAxisLabel "Density"@en ; cd:yAxisUnit "1" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l5-t16f-chart a cd:ChartDefinition ; skos:prefLabel "Normal versus Student-t with 10 and 30 degrees of freedom"@en ; cd:body "Solid: normal. Dashed: 10 degrees of freedom. Dotted: 30. Same axes; closer curves are not exact equality."@en ; cd:chartType cd:LineChart ; cd:chartPresentation "complete" ; cd:showChartPoints false ; cd:chartYMinimum 0.0 ; cd:chartYMaximum 0.42 ; cd:usesDataset ex:l5-c14-standard-normal ; dct:hasPart ex:l5-t16-t10, ex:l5-t16-t30 ; cd:xAxisLabel "Standardized statistic"@en ; cd:xAxisUnit "1" ; cd:yAxisLabel "Density"@en ; cd:yAxisUnit "1" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l5-t16g-chart a cd:ChartDefinition ; skos:prefLabel "Estimated SE versus 95% confidence-interval margin"@en ; cd:body "SE 0.141421; margin 0.392649 mg/L. Under iid normal assumptions, 95% describes long-run coverage of the procedure."@en ; cd:chartType cd:BarChart ; cd:chartPresentation "complete" ; cd:usesDataset ex:l5-t16-margin-data ; cd:xAxisLabel "Different quantities"@en ; cd:yAxisLabel "Concentration"@en ; cd:yAxisUnit "mg/L" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l5-t16-margin-data a cd:Dataset ; skos:prefLabel "Estimated laboratory SE and analytical t interval margin"@en ; cd:derivedFromResource ex:l5-s15-readings, ex:l5-t16g-formula ; cd:hasObservation ex:l5-t16-est-se, ex:l5-t16-margin ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l5-t16-est-se a cd:Observation ; cd:position 1 ; skos:prefLabel "Estimated SE"@en ; cd:numericValue 0.1414213562373095 ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l5-t16-margin a cd:Observation ; cd:position 2 ; skos:prefLabel "95% margin"@en ; cd:numericValue 0.39264863229551217 ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l5-t16-protocol a cd:WorkedExample ; cd:bodyFormat "markdown" ; cd:body """Continue S15's specified iid normal teaching model, mean 10 mg/L and variance 0.10 (mg/L) squared. Replay exactly its seed and draws, not an independent new collection: 5,000 fresh five-value samples, Mulberry32 seed 20261031, uncached cosine Box-Muller, two uniforms per draw. The displayed five bars are the first five variances in order, not selected extrema or a histogram. They are not the variance of S15's constructed symmetric observations. The chi-square curve is theoretical, not fitted to these five bars.

```javascript
function varianceSimulation() {
  let state=20261031;
  function uniform(){state=(state+0x6d2b79f5)>>>0;let t=state;t=Math.imul(t^(t>>>15),t|1);t^=t+Math.imul(t^(t>>>7),t|61);return((t^(t>>>14))>>>0)/4294967296;}
  const means=[],variances=[];
  for(let r=0;r<5000;r++){
    const row=[];
    for(let i=0;i<5;i++)row.push(10+Math.sqrt(.1)*Math.sqrt(-2*Math.log(1-uniform()))*Math.cos(2*Math.PI*uniform()));
    const mean=row.reduce((a,b)=>a+b,0)/5;
    means.push(mean);variances.push(row.reduce((a,x)=>a+(x-mean)**2,0)/4);
  }
  return {means,variances};
}
```

All line coordinates are theoretical density values, rounded to twelve decimal places and joined linearly, not raw observations or fitted curves. Chi-square with four degrees of freedom has density x exp(-x/2)/4 for nonnegative dimensionless x. Student-t with nu degrees of freedom has density Gamma((nu+1)/2)/(sqrt(nu*pi)*Gamma(nu/2)) times (1+x*x/nu)^(-(nu+1)/2). The normal reference is reused from C14. Plot windows do not bound support or imply renormalization. T curves use nu 2, 4, 10 and 30 on identical axes.

For the optional interval, use the S15 constructed observations, not a simulated sample: mean 10, sample variance 0.10, estimated SE sqrt(0.10/5). The Student-t cumulative-probability-0.975 quantile for four degrees of freedom is 2.7764451051977987; margin 0.39264863229551217 mg/L and endpoints 9.607351367704488 and 10.392648632295512 mg/L. Quantiles refer to the full theoretical distribution, not integrals of the piecewise-linear drawing. Under iid normal sampling the interval procedure has exact 95% coverage. This does not assign 95% probability to the fixed unknown population mean after observing this particular interval; it is not an individual-reading prediction interval and does not correct systematic bias."""@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
}

<https://w3id.org/project-chemie-digital/graph/paths/level5-binomial-opening> {
  ex:path-level5-binomial-opening cd:forTopic ex:l5-student-t ; cd:hasStep ex:l5-t16a-step, ex:l5-t16b-step, ex:l5-t16c-step, ex:l5-t16d-step, ex:l5-t16e-step, ex:l5-t16f-step, ex:l5-t16g-step .
  ex:l5-t16a-step a cd:PathStep ; cd:position 47 ; cd:usesScene ex:l5-t16a-scene ; cd:usesResource ex:l5-t16a-title, ex:l5-t16a-chart, ex:l5-t16a-formula ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l5-t16b-step a cd:PathStep ; cd:position 48 ; cd:usesScene ex:l5-t16b-scene ; cd:usesResource ex:l5-t16b-title, ex:l5-t16b-chart, ex:l5-t16b-formula ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l5-t16c-step a cd:PathStep ; cd:position 50 ; cd:usesScene ex:l5-t16c-scene ; cd:usesResource ex:l5-t16c-title, ex:l5-t16c-chart, ex:l5-t16c-formula ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l5-t16d-step a cd:PathStep ; cd:position 51 ; cd:usesScene ex:l5-t16d-scene ; cd:usesResource ex:l5-t16d-title, ex:l5-t16d-chart, ex:l5-t16d-formula ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l5-t16e-step a cd:PathStep ; cd:position 52 ; cd:usesScene ex:l5-t16e-scene ; cd:usesResource ex:l5-t16e-title, ex:l5-t16e-context, ex:l5-t16e-chart ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l5-t16f-step a cd:PathStep ; cd:position 53 ; cd:usesScene ex:l5-t16f-scene ; cd:usesResource ex:l5-t16f-title, ex:l5-t16f-context, ex:l5-t16f-chart ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l5-t16g-step a cd:PathStep ; cd:position 54 ; cd:usesScene ex:l5-t16g-scene ; cd:usesResource ex:l5-t16g-title, ex:l5-t16g-chart, ex:l5-t16g-formula ; cd:authoredResource true .
}
<https://w3id.org/project-chemie-digital/graph/scenes/level5-student-t> {
  ex:l5-t16a-scene a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:l5-student-t ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:l5-t16a-heading, ex:l5-t16a-plot, ex:l5-t16a-math ; cd:narration "T16a. A sample variance is itself random. Replay S15's same normal simulation and take the first five samples in order. Each new five-value sample gives a different variance: about 0.05217, 0.01428, 0.04345, 0.03829 and 0.06767 in squared concentration units. These are five sample results, not a histogram or the constructed S15 sample's variance of 0.10. The specified population variance is 0.10. Under independent identically distributed normal observations, multiplying random sample variance by n minus one and dividing by population variance gives an exact chi-square distribution with n minus one degrees of freedom. For size five that is four. The sample deviations sum to zero because they use an estimated mean, leaving four independent residual directions. We connect to the existing variance correction without deriving it again. The raw residuals are not five independent standard normals; independent normal coordinates in the residual subspace explain the four squared terms. This exact law is a normal-model result, not a consequence of the CLT for every five-value sample."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l5-t16b-scene a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:l5-student-t ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:l5-t16b-heading, ex:l5-t16b-plot, ex:l5-t16b-math ; cd:narration "The chi-square construction explains the preceding variance reference. Sum squares of independent standard-normal variables; squaring makes each dimensionless term nonnegative. Four independent terms give four degrees of freedom and the plotted right-skewed density. Its tail continues beyond 32 and it is not a finite observed histogram or a fitted model. The four degrees of freedom link to scaled variance from five iid normal laboratory measurements, not only four observations. Orthogonal normal residual coordinates, not the original dependent centered residuals treated as independent, justify that link. This reference helps account for uncertainty in estimated analytical spread before constructing a mean interval. It does not establish normal environmental concentrations. Pearson's later chi-square application concerns approximate category-count discrepancy, not this exact normal-sample variance law."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l5-t16c-scene a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:l5-student-t ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:l5-t16c-heading, ex:l5-t16c-plot, ex:l5-t16c-math ; cd:narration "T16c. First suppose individual population SD is genuinely known. Subtract the fixed population mean from the random sample mean and divide by its true sampling SD, population SD over square root of sample size. With iid normal observations, the resulting statistic is exactly standard normal for any positive size. There is no estimated random SD in this denominator. The standard normal reference has dimensionless mean zero and variance one, and both tails extend beyond the display. This is the known-SD case, not permission to use a normal cutoff unchanged after estimating SD from five observations. For nonnormal individuals the exact finite-sample statement need not hold; asymptotic results require their own assumptions."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l5-t16d-scene a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:l5-student-t ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:l5-t16d-heading, ex:l5-t16d-plot, ex:l5-t16d-math ; cd:narration "T16d. Population SD is usually unknown. Replacing it by the random sample SD makes the denominator random. Under iid normal sampling, the sample mean and sample variance are independent. Combining the standard-normal numerator with the independent chi-square variance scaling produces a Student-t statistic with n minus one degrees of freedom. In words, divide a standard normal by the square root of an independent chi-square variable divided by its degrees of freedom. This independence and normal sampling are essential to the exact derivation. At size five the reference has four degrees of freedom and heavier tails than standard normal. Heavier tails refer to greater tail probabilities, not every ordinate being higher. The solid normal and dashed t curves share identical axes; the t curve is lower at the center and higher far out. Their full supports extend beyond the window. This is an exact finite-sample model result, not a distribution selected merely because the sample is small."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l5-t16e-scene a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:l5-student-t ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:l5-t16e-heading, ex:l5-t16e-text, ex:l5-t16e-plot ; cd:narration "T16e. Compare the standard normal with Student-t at two and four degrees of freedom. Solid is normal, dashed is two, dotted is four. At low degrees of freedom the extra denominator variability gives more probability to the tails and less density near zero. All are symmetric, and no finite window contains their full support. The same horizontal domain minus six to six and vertical density domain zero to 0.42 will be retained on the next comparison. Degrees of freedom are distribution parameters, not concentration units. For a one-sample mean under the iid normal model, two and four degrees correspond to sample sizes three and five. The t distribution with two degrees of freedom does not have finite variance, so these are not a family of unit-variance densities."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l5-t16f-scene a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:l5-student-t ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:l5-t16f-heading, ex:l5-t16f-text, ex:l5-t16f-plot ; cd:narration "T16f. Keep the same axes and normal reference. The dashed Student-t curve now has ten degrees of freedom and the dotted curve thirty. They are closer to standard normal as the variance estimate becomes more stable. Student-t converges to standard normal as degrees of freedom increase without bound, but neither ten nor thirty gives exact equality. This is not a universal sample-size-thirty rule and does not establish normality, independence or reliable inference for arbitrary data. At thirty degrees of freedom in the one-sample normal model, sample size would be thirty-one, not thirty. Tail differences can still matter for a specified probability even when central curves nearly overlap."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l5-t16g-scene a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:l5-student-t ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:l5-t16g-heading, ex:l5-t16g-plot, ex:l5-t16g-math ; cd:narration "Use the five constructed laboratory values 9.6, 9.8, 10.0, 10.2 and 10.4 mg/L, not the environmental sample or a selected simulation series. Their mean is 10.0 and estimated SE 0.1414213562 mg/L. Under iid normal analytical errors, a two-sided 95 percent t interval uses four degrees of freedom and the full-distribution 0.975 quantile 2.7764451052, leaving 2.5 percent in each tail. Multiplication gives margin 0.3926486323 mg/L and endpoints about 9.60735 to 10.39265 mg/L. The margin, estimated SE and individual analytical SD have different meanings. Across repeated laboratory series satisfying the assumptions, the procedure covers the common fixed analytical expectation 95 percent of the time. We do not assign 95 percent probability to the fixed mean after seeing this interval, and it is not a prediction interval for individual values. Crucially, this is not a confidence interval for the water-body mean. It excludes environmental sampling variation and does not establish representativeness, normality or independence. Without bias control it does not automatically cover the true concentration of the collected sample either. The field investigation needs a design-matched uncertainty calculation; this laboratory interval cannot substitute for it."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
